================================================================================
UPSTREAM WORK
================================================================================

This project began from and is derived from:

    OpenDrop
    Copyright (C) Andrew Brown
    https://github.com/WanderingAstronomer/OpenDrop
    Licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0

OpenDrop is an open-data map of clothing-donation drop-off points. Find a Food
Truck was started by cloning it and reworking it for a different subject. Used
both under the terms of the AGPL and with the author's express written
permission.

The AGPL is why this project is also AGPL: section 5(c) requires that a derived
work be released under the same license. The permission from the author is in
addition to that grant, not a substitute for it, and does not remove the
attribution and source-availability obligations below.

WHAT CARRIED OVER, AND WHAT DID NOT

  Carried over: the overall shape of the thing — a Postgres/PostGIS schema
  behind a no-build Leaflet frontend, a numeric confidence score attached to
  each mapped point, an anonymous crowd-reporting path gated server-side, and a
  pending-submissions queue that never writes the public table directly.

  Reworked: the core model is inverted. OpenDrop tracks fixed points whose
  EXISTENCE decays over months; this tracks moving vendors whose LOCATION is
  true for a few hours. That changes the confidence function from a stored
  column into a query-time function parameterized by `as_of`, and it introduces
  schedules, appearances and sightings, which have no OpenDrop equivalent.

  The schema, the confidence model, the frontend and the write API in this
  repository were written for this project. The debt is architectural, and it is
  a real debt: this exists because OpenDrop showed the shape first.


================================================================================
SECTION 13 — NETWORK USE
================================================================================

The AGPL differs from the GPL in one respect that matters for a hosted map:
running this software on a server and letting the public interact with it over a
network counts as conveying it. If you deploy a modified version, section 13
obliges you to offer those users the corresponding source of YOUR version.

In practice: keep a visible link from the deployed site to the source of the
exact revision it is running.


